  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper uses dormant harmful-behavior activation as the motivation and threat model for a defense, and reports attack and defense results. Its harmful behaviors are deliberately implanted by poisoning; under the study rules, activation of that induced behavior does not test whether ordinary fine-tuning erodes safety in otherwise aligned models. It does not test the token-position, bypass, localization, or deepening claims.",
    "gate_note": "The vanilla-fine-tuning rows below report harmful behavior after benign downstream fine-tuning of models deliberately poisoned to activate that behavior. They are recorded as findings about induced dormant poisoning, not as tests of the claim versions. The defense results are not cataloged as rows because they measure the proposed defense rather than the claim's mechanism.",
    "test_reason": "This is mainly a defense paper against deliberately implanted dormant poisoning. Rows r1–r23 measure induced behaviour and do not test any version. One incidental control does bear on V2. Table 6(c) fine-tunes the clean, unpoisoned Qwen3-4B-Instruct on benign GSM8K with vanilla FT, and HarmBench ASR rises from 0.5% to 9.5%. That is a single-model result from a new setting on benign fine-tuning eroding safety, and no row records it.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that dormant poisoning extends to language and multimodal agents, transfers to unseen actions, and lets multiple behaviours coexist in one model. They conclude that LookAhead Defense suppresses activation with low over-refusal and competitive utility. They do not comment on the 9.5% ASR rise in the clean checkpoint.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper adapts dormant poisoning to agent actions and evaluates defenses against it; it does not reproduce the fine-tuning-safety or token-depth methods in the cited original sources.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported activation varies by model, risk category, and evaluation benchmark; multimodal activation on RiOSWorld-Unseen differs across the two models.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Benign downstream fine-tuning activates harmful behaviour that was deliberately implanted by FAB or Agentic FAB poisoning. This holds for language models (HarmBench ASR up to 92.5%), language agents and multimodal agents, and extends to unseen tools and risk categories.",
        "proposed_claim": "Benign downstream fine-tuning can activate harmful behaviour intentionally implanted in a model beforehand, including harmful agent actions.",
