    "problems": "No uncertainty estimates are reported for the tabulated outcomes. Experiments are limited to models up to 8B parameters, as the authors note. The reported harmful activations follow deliberate poisoning and do not establish spontaneous safety erosion in unpoisoned models.",
    "rows_left_out": "Defense-comparison and utility outcomes are not included because they measure the proposed defense or downstream task performance rather than the claim's mechanism. Repeated aggregate activation summaries are omitted where the category- or benchmark-level rows already report the outcomes.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No harmful-data fine-tuning of an unpoisoned safety-trained model."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "Only one unpoisoned model (Qwen3-4B-Instruct), one dataset (GSM8K) and one run length (2,000 steps) are tested. There are no other benign datasets, no data-size or step sweep, no agent settings on clean models, and no 'carefully chosen' benign data apart from the deliberate Benign DPO attack."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No per-token KL or response-position analysis."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No prefilling, suffix or decoding attacks."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No localization of safety in weights or ranks."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No analysis of why alignment is shallow."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "No token-depth safety training. LookAhead is a fine-tuning-time penalty, not a deepening of alignment."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [
      {
        "what": "Clean (unpoisoned) Qwen3-4B-Instruct, GSM8K vanilla FT: HarmBench ASR 0.5 before FT vs 9.5 after; over-refusal 4.8 vs 3.6; LookAhead gives ASR 0.0. Add a V2 row with baseline 0.5, value 9.5, state observed.",
        "locator": "Table 6(c), Section 5.4",
        "versions": "V2"
      }
    ],
    "verdict_notes": "The reader's T4/uses_without_test is reasonable for the main contribution but overlooks the clean-checkpoint control, which is the only V2-relevant evidence; I classified the study as tests/T3 on the strength of that single result. Sleeper and Benign DPO are deliberate attacks, so they are not V2 evidence. Their vanilla-FT rows in Table 5 show little change in ASR and are left out correctly. The paper's own Table 3(a) 'clean checkpoint' column refers to the source of the Safety Buffer, not to clean-model ASR. No uncertainty is reported."
  },
